Package: os7-app-containers
Version: @OS7_VERSION@
Architecture: @OS7_ARCH@
Maintainer: Bastian Wirth <bastian.wirth@upinblue.com>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: dotnet-runtime-10.0, libfontconfig1, libx11-6, libice6, libsm6, polkitd, pkexec, os7-module (= @OS7_VERSION@)
Recommends: os7-desktop-theme (= @OS7_VERSION@)
Homepage: https://github.com/upinblue/os7
Description: OS/7 Containers — the machine's container engine, in a window
 Computer Management's idiom for one Docker engine (docs/CONTAINERS-PLAN.md):
 a tree of what the engine holds, a list, the selected object's properties and
 a status line. The default view is what needs attention, not what is there.
 .
 It implements no policy. Which containers need attention, who can reach a
 published port, what a clean-up would remove and which of it is safe, whether
 a registry has moved a tag on — every one of those is an OS7 cmdlet's answer,
 reached through one verb table that the root helper reaches too. The details
 pane renders the cmdlet's object as it came, so a property a cmdlet gains
 appears here with no change to the application.
 .
 It never runs as root. An account an administrator made a container operator
 talks to the engine directly; anybody else signs in through polkit, and the
 helper behind it runs exactly one cmdlet per verb. Seeing and operating
 remember the password for a few minutes; removing asks every time. pkexec is
 a Depends because the two helpers are started through it and Ubuntu ships it
 as a package of its own.
 .
 It installs no engine. Install-OS7ContainerHost does that, from PowerShell,
 with the engine's two stores on datasets of their own first.
 .
 amd64 only. arm64 is server-only and has no desktop; the cmdlets work there.
